<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!--
  Publishes the `enforceOrderOwnership` custom attribute from the
  biscuit-coffee-agent API Product so Biscuit-Coffee-Shop can honour it.

  WHY IT LIVES HERE
  Same reason as AM-MaxOrderAmount: product attributes only materialise as flow
  variables inside the proxy that ran VerifyAPIKey, and mcp-proxy-prod is the
  only one that does. Biscuit-Coffee-Shop authenticates with a Keycloak JWT and
  has no VerifyAPIKey policy at all, so the value has to be read here and
  forwarded on the wire as a header.

  WHY PRODUCT LEVEL, NOT OPERATION LEVEL
  maxOrderAmount is an operation attribute because it only applies to
  placeOrder. Ownership spans three operations - getOrder, cancelOrder and
  listOrders - so an operation attribute would mean flipping three switches on
  stage and getting a half-enforced API if you missed one. A single
  product-level attribute is one switch with no inconsistent intermediate
  state.

  WHY THE TEMPLATE CONCATENATES TWO VARIABLES
  Exactly one of the two VerifyAPIKey policies executes per request, and the
  one that did not run leaves its variable unresolved, which renders as an
  empty string. The concatenation therefore always yields the single real
  value. (Same trick as AM-MaxOrderAmount; AssignVariable's Value element is a
  literal, not a template, which is why this has to be done in a Template.)

  FAIL CLOSED
  Nothing here needs to default to "true". An absent, empty or unrecognised
  header is treated as ENFORCE by checkOrderOwnership.js, which only disables
  the check for the exact string "false". So a missing attribute, a
  misconfigured product, or a direct REST caller that never passed through this
  proxy all stay protected.
-->
<AssignMessage continueOnError="false" enabled="true" name="AM-OwnershipFlag">
  <DisplayName>AM-OwnershipFlag</DisplayName>
  <AssignVariable>
    <Name>ownership.enforce</Name>
    <Template>{verifyapikey.VA-VerifyKey.apiproduct.enforceOrderOwnership}{verifyapikey.VA-VerifyKey-Header.apiproduct.enforceOrderOwnership}</Template>
  </AssignVariable>
  <Set>
    <Headers>
      <Header name="X-Enforce-Order-Ownership">{ownership.enforce}</Header>
      <!-- Proves to Biscuit-Coffee-Shop that these policy headers were set by
           this proxy and not by the client.

           Biscuit-Coffee-Shop is directly reachable at prod.apigee-demo.com
           with nothing more than an ordinary customer token, so without this
           the headers are attacker-controlled: a customer could send
           `X-Enforce-Order-Ownership: false` to switch off the ownership check,
           or `X-Max-Order-Amount: 999999` to lift their own spending cap. Both
           were confirmed against the live gateway before this was added.

           Covers BOTH policy headers, which is why it is set here rather than
           in AM-MaxOrderAmount: this policy runs last, so one header carries
           the trust for everything the product publishes.

           Must stay in step with POLICY_SIGNATURE in checkOrderOwnership.js and
           checkOrderValue.js. In production all three should read it from an
           encrypted KVM instead of carrying a literal. -->
      <Header name="X-Policy-Signature">bcs-gw-policy-9d41f7a2c6be4815</Header>
    </Headers>
  </Set>

  <AssignTo createNew="false" transport="http" type="request"/>
  <IgnoreUnresolvedVariables>true</IgnoreUnresolvedVariables>
</AssignMessage>
